  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 113 PM MST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nObservations show skies starting to clear up over the foothills and \nurban corridor, with some cloud cover lingering over the eastern \nplains. Temperatures should start warming up as clouds continue to \nlift, with highs expected to be in the 50s for most of the \nplains/urban corridor and 40s in the mountains. Areas where cloud \ncover is expected to stick around longer (mainly Lincoln County and \nsouthern Elbert County) will stay cooler, with highs in the upper \n40s/low 50s.\n\nFog development is expected again tomorrow morning over the eastern \nplains and pushing into the urban corridor. Highest confidence for \nvisibility less than 2SM is over Lincoln County and southern Weld \nCounty into Morgan County, though isolated visibility reductions \nover the plains and the Denver metro area can't be ruled out. Once \nfog/low clouds lift, temperatures will warm back up and stay warmer \nthan average through Tuesday thanks to an upper-level ridge moving \neast across Colorado. Highs will climb into the mid 50s in the \nmountain valleys and the low 60s across the plains & urban corridor \nboth days.\n\nThings get a bit more interesting on Wednesday, with a shortwave \nentering the area and bringing rain/snow showers to the \nmountains. At the start of the system late Tuesday night, \nelevations above 8500ft will see snow, with lower valleys seeing \nrain or a rain/snow mix. This should transition to all snow as the\nshortwave moves eastward Wednesday morning. Accumulations are \nexpected to be low, only a trace to a couple hundredths, though \nthe highest peaks could see up to an inch. With the progression of\nthe shortwave, cross-barrier westerly flow aloft and strong \nsubsidence could create favorable conditions for a high wind event\nfor the Front Range Wednesday morning. The current forecast has \nwind gusts reaching 60-70mph at the highest peaks, with more \nwidespread gusts of 35-45mph along the Front Range. Multiple \nmodels have also started indicating the potential for a stable \nlayer between 700-500mb, which, coupled with the cross-barrier \nflow and subsidence, could be a favorable environment for a \nmountain wave. We'll be watching this and the other ingredients \nfor high wind/mountain wave events as we get closer to Wednesday \nmorning. \n\nThursday should be cool and dry behind the shortwave, with the next \nsystem moving in over the weekend. Recent runs favor the more open \ntrough solution, with the bulk of precipitation staying west of our \nCWA. Ensembles continue to trend downward for precipitation chances \nover the plains, though the mountains could still see some snow. \nMountain snow could start late Thursday and continue through mid \nSaturday, and for now the best chance for precip over the plains \nlooks to be late Friday. At this time, the chance for snow \naccumulations over the plains is low, about 10% chance or less for a \ntrace.",
